# programming-vue.kb - Programming / Vue
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 4 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
vue | definition | Vue is a progressive JavaScript framework, which means the same library works in three ways: as plain script tags that add reactivity to an existing page, as modules for a larger build, or as a full single file component setup. Its centre is reactive state, where declaring a value inside a reactive object or a ref automatically tracks anything that reads it and updates only what depends on it, so a template updates without manual refresh code. The template syntax is deliberately close to plain HTML with a few extra attributes like v-model for two way form binding, v-if for conditional rendering and v-for for lists, and the learning curve is short for anyone who has used HTML. The single file component convention packs the template, the script and the style for one component into one file, which many teams find easier to read than separating them. It is a smaller and gentler framework than Angular, and unlike React it is opinionated enough to give you a standard structure to follow. | progressive framework, reactivity, single file component, template syntax, v-model, v-if, virtual dom | what is vue, is vue hard to learn, vue vs react | Vue | Vue
reactive | definition | Reactivity in Vue is the mechanism that makes a user interface update when data changes, and the key difference from a plain variable is that Vue tracks who read a value. When you return a plain object from setup, Vue wraps it in a Proxy that records property access, and when you write to a property it notifies exactly the components whose render functions read that property. Refs are the equivalent for a single value, where reading .value inside script gives you the tracked read and the template unwraps it automatically. A computed property caches a derived value and recomputes only when its dependencies change, which is the right tool for a value that would otherwise be recalculated on every render. The reason the tracking works is contrast with a non reactive variable: a plain let in a function body is invisible to Vue, and mutating it changes nothing on screen. That is also why you are warned against destructuring a reactive object into plain variables, because the tracking link is lost. | proxy, ref, computed, dependency tracking, dependency graph, watch, mutation | how does vue reactivity work, what is a ref in vue, what is a computed property | reactive | reactive
vue directive | definition | A Vue directive is an attribute that extends an element's behaviour rather than its content, and the built-in ones cover most needs. v-model is the most used: it wires an input or textarea to a value in both directions so typing updates the data and changing the data updates the field. v-if and v-else-if and v-else remove an element from the DOM entirely when the condition is false, while v-show keeps the element and toggles a display style, and the difference matters when the element is expensive to create or holds state. v-for renders a list, taking a value and a key, and that key matters because it is how Vue tracks which element is which when the list is reordered, and using the array index there causes a well known class of state bugs. v-on handles events and v-bind passes attributes, and custom directives are written with the directive lifecycle hooks when something genuinely has no computed equivalent. | v-model, v-if, v-show, v-for, v-bind, v-on, key | what are vue directives, difference between v-if and v-show, why does v-for need a key | vue directive | vue directive
vue router | definition | The Vue Router is the official routing library, and it maps the URL path to a component so a single page application can show different views without a full page load. Routes are declared as a list, each with a path, a component and optionally a name and parameters, and the router renders whichever one matches the current address. Its two modes are the history mode, which uses the pushState API and needs a server rewrite so a deep link serves index.html, and the hash mode, which puts a hash in the URL and needs no server configuration at all. Dynamic segments are declared with a colon, so a path of /user/:id matches many concrete URLs and gives the component the id as a parameter, while a catch-all segment is how a not-found view is expressed. Navigation guards are the feature people reach for first, because they let you redirect an unauthenticated user before the component mounts. The critical caution is that an unmatched path should render a deliberate not-found view rather than a blank page. | spa, route, navigation guard, pushstate, url parameter, deep link, not found | what is vue router, how does vue router work, what is a navigation guard | vue router | vue router
